experiment: bitsnr
  the question      §45. tutorgrid's F1 bit loses 57% over four
                    generations and its F2 bit loses nothing. Is the F1
                    bit simply a WEAKER SIGNAL IN THE AIR?
  the unit          Hz is the wrong one. F1 carries 2.06x more d' PER HZ
                    than F2 (§43: 7.857 at 118.0 vs 9.722 at 300.3), so
                    tutorgrid's 37.6 vs 91.9 Hz is 2.44x in Hz and about
                    1.19x in airborne d'. Measured here, not extrapolated.
  the jitter        kAESdG, §43's audible-tick values -- the denominator
                    measured where the creature is actually sounding.
  the level         0.303, the amplitude the creature hears itself at

  CLAUSE 1 -- WIRING: at each axis's OWN reach this must return §43's
  values. An instrument that cannot reproduce the table it extends cannot
  extend it -- and reproducing a KNOWN axis difference is also what proves
  clause 2 is answerable either way.
    f1  reach  118.0 Hz   d'  7.857 +- 0.093   §43 says  7.857   -0.000  -> ok
    f2  reach  300.3 Hz   d'  9.722 +- 0.094   §43 says  9.722   -0.000  -> ok

  CLAUSE 2 -- AT THE DELIVERED SEPARATIONS (tutorgrid run 3, generation 0)
    f1    37.6 Hz (the bit that DECAYS 57%)   d'  2.576 +- 0.065
    f2    91.9 Hz (the bit that holds      )   d'  2.708 +- 0.045

  CLAUSE 3 -- POWER, read BEFORE the contrast: SE on the gap 0.079 against
  the pre-registered ceiling 0.16  -> adequate
    gap  d'(F2) - d'(F1)  +0.132 +- 0.079  (1.7 SE)   ratio 1.051
    -> EQUAL WITHIN ERROR -- THE AIRBORNE-SNR ACCOUNT IS REFUTED,
       MODEL-FREE: two bits on one creature, same jitter, equally
       audible, one decaying 57% and the other not at all cannot
       differ because one is the weaker signal

  WHAT THIS CANNOT DO: explain the decay. It removes, or fails to remove,
  the one surviving named cause. If the verdict is EQUAL then the lead has
  no candidate answers left, which is a worse-sounding and more useful
  position than one with two bad ones.
